{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm.semantic on dao.stream — linear executable datoms\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: Phase 0 contract. Sections §1–§6 are promoted verbatim from\n     4\t`collab/1789221648668-architect-semantic-vm-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`.\n     5\tThe opcode table (§2.4) and the attribute tables (§2.2, §2.3) are the\n     6\tcontract that `yin.vm/code-schema`, `yin.vm/opcode-table`, and\n     7\t`yin.vm.code/well-formed?` implement. The integration and roadmap\n     8\tsections (§7, §8) remain in the findings note.\n     9\t\n    10\t---\n    11\t\n    12\t## §1. Foundational axioms and invariant compliance\n    13\t\n    14\t### 1.1 The four axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t**Everything is a Stream.** Code arrives on a program stream as a segment\n    17\tvalue; execution is consumption of that stream by an observer, and then\n    18\tconsumption of the segment by the machine. Effects the program performs\n    19\t(`:stream-put`, `:stream-next`, FFI) are emissions on streams the composition\n    20\tsupplied, exactly as for the walker. Optional execution traces are emissions\n    21\ttoo (§3.6). What is *not* a stream is the instruction fetch inside a loaded\n    22\tsegment, and this is deliberate: a boundary belongs \"wherever you need\n    23\tdecoupling — and nowhere else\" (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §1). Fetch has\n    24\tone consumer, one order, no replay need, and no host boundary; putting a\n    25\tstream there is the per-value tax §4 of that note names as hopeless.\n    26\t\n    27\t**Interpretation Creates Semantics.** `[e :yin.code/op :call]` means nothing\n    28\tuntil `yin.vm.semantic` reads it. The same datoms can be read by a query\n    29\t(\"all call sites of this closure\"), a renderer (back to source through\n    30\t`:yin.code/source`), a verifier (every block ends in a terminator), or a\n    31\tdifferent VM. The mapping from the keyword mnemonic in the datom to the\n    32\tinteger opcode in the image is the loader's interpretation, not a fact in\n    33\tthe datom. `:yin.code/tail?` is a property of a call in the datoms; only the\n    34\timage decides it is a distinct `tailcall` dispatch.\n    35\t\n    36\t**Code and State are Datoms.** Instructions are entities with attributes.\n    37\tExecution state is plain data: the control is `{:segment id :pc n}`, the\n    38\tenvironment a map, the operand stack a vector, the continuation a vector of\n    39\tframes each `{:pc :env :stack-base :segment}`. The optional trace vocabulary\n    40\t(§3.6) deposits exactly these as `[e a v t m]` with `t` the step counter,\n    41\tthe \"endgame\" of `cesk-space-optimization.md` §6: execute fast, emit the\n    42\tmachine image as datoms rather than interpret from them.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Everything is a Continuation.** Park writes `{segment pc env stack k}` into\n    45\t`:parked`; resume reads it back. Nothing in that map is a host object except\n    46\tvalues the program itself put in `env` or on the stack (host functions\n    47\tresolved from primitives, stream handles from the store), which is the same\n    48\tserialization limit the walker has today. A continuation shipped through a\n    49\tstream names its segment by id; the receiving host resolves the id against\n    50\tsegment datoms it holds or was sent alongside.\n    51\t\n    52\t### 1.2 The six invariants\n    53\t\n    54\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    55\t| Invariant                | How the design honors it                                                         |\n    56\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    57\t| No hidden global state   | Opcode table is a value in `yin.vm`. Segments live in the VM record under     |\n    58\t|                          | `:code`. Primitives, modules, `:make-stream`, bridge handlers are all supplied   |\n    59\t|                          | at construction, as for the walker. No `defonce`, no registry.                   |\n    60\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    61\t| No implicit control flow | Every transfer of control is an instruction datom: `:jump`, `:branch-false`,     |\n    62\t|                          | `:call`, `:return`, `:halt`. Sequencing is the explicit `:yin.code/pc` order. No |\n    63\t|                          | fallthrough that is not a `pc + 1` visible in the datoms.                        |\n    64\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    65\t| No callbacks             | Host effects go through `engine/handle-effect` and the FFI request/response      |\n    66\t|                          | pair; a blocked read parks in the polling wait set. The VM invokes nothing and   |\n    67\t|                          | is invoked by nothing.                                                           |\n    68\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    69\t| No shared mutable state  | The image array is built once per segment and never written after load. The hot  |\n    70\t|                          | loop keeps registers in `loop` locals; the persistent VM record is the only      |\n    71\t|                          | state that escapes a step. No mutable deftype fields (a cljd trap on record).    |\n    72\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    73\t| No layer collapsing      | Lowering (AST datoms → code datoms), loading (code datoms → image), and          |\n    74\t|                          | execution (image → transitions) are three functions in two namespaces with data  |\n    75\t|                          | between each. The walker keeps interpreting the AST; nothing in the walker       |\n    76\t|                          | changes.                                                                         |\n    77\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    78\t| No assumed graphs        | Branch targets and closure bodies are refs; the loader constructs the pc index   |\n    79\t|                          | from tuples in an explicit pass and resolves refs against it. A dangling ref is  |\n    80\t|                          | a load error naming the entity, never a runtime lookup.                          |\n    81\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    82\t\n    83\t---\n    84\t\n    85\t## §2. The linear executable datom specification\n    86\t\n    87\t### 2.1 Tuple format\n    88\t\n    89\tExecutable datoms are canonical 5-tuples `[e a v t m]`, produced the same\n    90\tway `yin.vm/ast->datoms` produces AST datoms: negative tempids for `e`,\n    91\t`t` = 0 unless the linearizer is given one, `m` = `dao.datom/default-op`.\n    92\tAttributes live under the `:yin.code/*` namespace so that\n    93\t`engine/executable-program-datom?` (which selects `:yin/*`) does not confuse\n    94\ta code segment with an AST program, and a query can select either view.\n    95\t\n    96\t### 2.2 The segment\n    97\t\n    98\tA segment is one entity plus its instructions. It is the unit a program\n    99\tstream carries (one batch value), the unit the loader accepts, and the unit\n   100\ta closure or continuation names.\n   101\t\n   102\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   103\t| Attribute                | Value              | Meaning                                                                          |\n   104\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   105\t| `:yin.code/type`         | `:segment`         | Marks the segment entity.                                                        |\n   106\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   107\t| `:yin.code/length`       | int                | Number of instructions; pcs are `0 .. length-1`, dense.                          |\n   108\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   109\t| `:yin.code/derived-from` | ref (AST root eid) | Provenance; optional when hand-assembled.                                        |\n   110\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   111\t| `:yin.code/hash`         | string             | Reserved: content address of the canonical instruction datoms, for dedup and     |\n   112\t|                          |                    | verification (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3). Not required in Phase 1.      |\n   113\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   114\t\n   115\tEntry is pc 0 by rule. Making entry a fact would let a segment disagree with\n   116\titself; a rule cannot.\n   117\t\n   118\t### 2.3 The instruction\n   119\t\n   120\tEvery instruction entity carries three structural facts and one or more\n   121\toperand facts.\n   122\t\n   123\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n   124\t| Attribute           | Value                    | Required on                                           |\n   125\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n   126\t| `:yin.code/segment` | ref (segment eid)        | every instruction                                     |\n   127\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n   128\t| `:yin.code/pc`      | int                      | every instruction                                     |\n   129\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n   130\t| `:yin.code/op`      | keyword mnemonic (below) | every instruction                                     |\n   131\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n   132\t| `:yin.code/source`  | ref (AST node eid)       | every lowered instruction; absent when hand-assembled |\n   133\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n   134\t\n   135\tLinearity is therefore an **explicit fact**, `:yin.code/pc`, not the vector\n   136\torder of the batch and not entity-id arithmetic. Entity ids are tempids that\n   137\ta transactor may remap; vector order is a transport accident. The\n   138\twell-formedness rule (§2.6) requires the batch to be *sorted* by pc so a\n   139\tloader can stream it, but the pc fact is the truth.\n   140\t\n   141\tBranch and closure targets are **refs to instruction entities**\n   142\t(`:yin.code/target`, `:yin.code/body`), not pc integers. The loader resolves\n   143\teach ref to a pc through the index it builds. This keeps the datom semantic\n   144\t(\"this branch goes to that instruction\"), survives id remapping when both\n   145\tends are remapped together, and is checkable.\n   146\t\n   147\t### 2.4 The core semantic primitives\n   148\t\n   149\tMachine model: an **accumulator machine with an operand stack** (§4). `val`\n   150\tis the accumulator; `St` the operand stack; `K` the continuation of return\n   151\tframes. This is the stack-class formulation `streams-all-the-way-down.md` §3\n   152\tsays survives the log-structured reading; a register model would not.\n   153\t\n   154\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   155\t| `:yin.code/op`          | Operand attributes                            | Effect on the machine                                                            |\n   156\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   157\t| `:const`                | `:yin.code/value v`                           | `val ← v`                                                                        |\n   158\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   159\t| `:var`                  | `:yin.code/name sym`                          | `val ← resolve(E, S, prims, modules, sym)`                                       |\n   160\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   161\t| `:closure`              | `:yin.code/params [..]`, `:yin.code/body ref` | `val ← {:type :closure :params .. :entry pc(body) :segment seg :env E}`          |\n   162\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   163\t| `:push`                 | —                                             | `St ← St ⧺ [val]`                                                                |\n   164\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   165\t| `:call`                 | `:yin.code/argc n`, `:yin.code/tail? bool`    | pop `n` args and the operator below them; apply (§4.3)                           |\n   166\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   167\t| `:return`               | —                                             | pop frame from `K`; restore `pc`, `E`, stack base; `val` unchanged; empty `K`    |\n   168\t|                         |                                               | halts                                                                            |\n   169\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   170\t| `:jump`                 | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← target`                                                                    |\n   171\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   172\t| `:branch-false`         | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← val ? pc+1 : target`                                                       |\n   173\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   174\t| `:halt`                 | —                                             | halt with `val` as the result                                                    |\n   175\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   176\t| `:gensym`               | `:yin.code/prefix`                            | `val ← fresh id`; id counter advances                                            |\n   177\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   178\t| `:store-get`            | `:yin.code/key`                               | `val ← S[key]`                                                                   |\n   179\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   180\t| `:store-put`            | `:yin.code/key`, `:yin.code/value`            | `S[key] ← v; val ← v`                                                            |\n   181\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   182\t| `:stream-make`          | `:yin.code/buffer`                            | effect `:stream/make` via engine                                                 |\n   183\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   184\t| `:stream-put`           | —                                             | target ref popped from `St`, value in `val`; effect `:stream/put`                |\n   185\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   186\t| `:stream-cursor`        | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/cursor`                                     |\n   187\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   188\t| `:stream-next`          | —                                             | cursor ref in `val`; effect `:stream/next`                                       |\n   189\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   190\t| `:stream-close`         | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/close`                                      |\n   191\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   192\t| `:park`                 | —                                             | `engine/park-continuation` with the current frame                                |\n   193\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   194\t| `:resume`               | `:yin.code/parked-id`                         | resume value in `val`; `engine/resume-continuation`                              |\n   195\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   196\t| `:current-continuation` | —                                             | `val ← {:type :reified-continuation :segment :pc :env :stack :k}`                |\n   197\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   198\t| `:ffi-call`             | `:yin.code/ffi-op kw`, `:yin.code/argc n`     | pop `n` args; park-and-call over the FFI pair                                    |\n   199\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   200\t\n   201\tThis is the AST vocabulary of `yin.vm/ast->datoms` made linear, one for\n   202\tone, plus the three sequencing primitives (`:push`, `:jump`, `:branch-false`)\n   203\tand `:halt`. `:yin/macro-expand` and `:vm/store-update` are outside both\n   204\tevaluators' supported corpus and are rejected at lowering with an error\n   205\tnaming the node.\n   206\t\n   207\t**Opcode integers.** `yin.vm/opcode-table` already assigns integers to\n   208\t`:literal :load-var :lambda :call :return :branch :jump :gensym :store-get\n   209\t:store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close\n   210\t:park :resume :current-cont :tailcall :dao.stream.apply/call`. The loader\n   211\tmaps mnemonics onto that table (`:const`→`:literal`, `:var`→`:load-var`,\n   212\t`:closure`→`:lambda`, `:branch-false`→`:branch`, `:ffi-call`→\n   213\t`:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:call`+`:tail? true`→`:tailcall`) and the table\n   214\tgains `:push 22` and `:halt 23`. `:move` stays unused. Keeping the datom\n   215\tmnemonics semantic and the image integers mechanical is the point: a query\n   216\tasks for `:call`, the dispatch switches on `5` or `20`.\n   217\t\n   218\t### 2.5 Constants and literals\n   219\t\n   220\tA literal lives inline in the `v` slot of `:yin.code/value`, polymorphic\n   221\texactly as `:yin/value` is today (numbers, strings, keywords, vectors, maps,\n   222\tsets, nil). Host functions never appear in code datoms; a primitive is named\n   223\tby symbol in `:var` and resolved at run time through the supplied\n   224\t`:primitives`, which is how the walker already avoids putting host objects in\n   225\tthe program. A shared or large constant pool (`:yin.code/const-ref` to a\n   226\tconstant entity) is reserved and not needed for the current corpus.\n   227\t\n   228\t### 2.6 Well-formedness (checked by the loader, tested in Phase 0)\n   229\t\n   230\t1. Exactly one entity with `:yin.code/type :segment` per batch.\n   231\t2. Instruction pcs are exactly `0 .. length-1`, each once.\n   232\t3. The batch is sorted by pc (so a loader can fill the array in one pass).\n   233\t4. Every `:yin.code/target` and `:yin.code/body` resolves to an instruction\n   234\t   of the same segment.\n   235\t5. Every basic block ends in a terminator (`:jump`, `:return`, `:halt`) or\n   236\t   falls into a labelled successor; pc `length-1` is a terminator.\n   237\t6. Every `:call`/`:ffi-call` has a non-negative `:yin.code/argc`.\n   238\t\n   239\tViolation is a load error naming the entity and rule. The loader is total\n   240\tover the outcomes of its inputs; it does not guess.\n   241\t\n   242\t### 2.7 A worked segment\n   243\t\n   244\t`((fn [x] (+ x 1)) 10)` lowers to (pcs shown, refs written as `→pc`):\n   245\t\n   246\t```\n   247\tseg   :yin.code/type :segment   :yin.code/length 14\n   248\t0  :closure   params [x]  body →6\n   249\t1  :push\n   250\t2  :const     10\n   251\t3  :push\n   252\t4  :call      argc 1  tail? false\n   253\t5  :halt\n   254\t6  :var       +               ; body of the lambda, out of line\n   255\t7  :push\n   256\t8  :var       x\n   257\t9  :push\n   258\t10 :const     1\n   259\t11 :push\n   260\t12 :call      argc 2  tail? true\n   261\t13 :return\n   262\t```\n   263\t\n   264\tThe main sequence (pcs 0–5) ends in `halt`; the body (pcs 6–13) ends in\n   265\t`return`. In datoms, pc 4 is:\n   266\t\n   267\t```\n   268\t[-20 :yin.code/segment -1  0 :db/add]\n   269\t[-20 :yin.code/pc       4  0 :db/add]\n   270\t[-20 :yin.code/op   :call  0 :db/add]\n   271\t[-20 :yin.code/argc     1  0 :db/add]\n   272\t[-20 :yin.code/tail? false 0 :db/add]\n   273\t[-20 :yin.code/source  -16 0 :db/add]   ; the :application AST node\n   274\t```\n   275\t\n   276\tSix datoms for a call site; five for most instructions. Load cost is linear\n   277\tin datoms once per segment. Execution cost is per instruction, never per\n   278\tdatom.\n   279\t\n   280\t---\n   281\t\n   282\t## §3. The semantic VM as a DaoStream v2 interpreter\n   283\t\n   284\t### 3.1 Consumption: observer, loader, image\n   285\t\n   286\tThe VM implements `IVM`; program ingress is exactly the walker's shape:\n   287\t\n   288\t- The host composes a program medium, attaches `yin.vm.stream-observer`\n   289\t  to it, and drives `observer/run-on-stream` with `engine/ready-for-ingress?`,\n   290\t  a loader, and `vm/run`. The observer owns the handle, the cursor, and gap\n   291\t  accounting. The VM holds no program stream.\n   292\t- The loader for this evaluator is `semantic/vm-load-program`: it accepts a\n   293\t  batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms, validates §2.6, builds the image, stores\n   294\t  it under `:code {segment-id image}`, and sets control to\n   295\t  `{:segment id :pc 0}`. A composition that carries `:yin/*` AST datoms on\n   296\t  its program stream hands the observer `(comp semantic/vm-load-program\n   297\t  linearize/lower)` instead; the loader is composition-supplied, so no\n   298\t  evaluator learns which form travels. `yin.repl.core/make-session` is\n   299\t  where that choice is made.\n   300\t\n   301\tThe step loop is then: `(aget code pc)` → integer `case` → transition. No\n   302\thandle, no cursor, no `next` inside a segment.\n   303\t\n   304\t### 3.2 Why a branch is not a cursor move\n   305\t\n   306\tThe contract's cursor is \"a position, not a claim\" and comes only from the\n   307\tstream: minted at `:oldest`/`:newest`, returned as a successor, or recovered\n   308\tfrom a `gap`. There is no `seek`, and reaching for a transport-owned position\n   309\tanchor is forbidden for contract-generic code. Three designs were weighed:\n   310\t\n   311\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   312\t| Design                                                                         | Contract status                                                             | Cost                                                                             | Verdict                                                                          |\n   313\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   314\t| Instruction stream, `next` per fetch, branch by re-minting a block's `:oldest` | legal only if every basic block is its own stream, delivered as descriptors | one stream operation per branch, one attach per closure body, per-value boundary | rejected: it is the per-scalar boundary §4 of the streams note names as          |\n   315\t|                                                                                |                                                                             | tax on every instruction                                                         | hopeless, and a loop body would re-mint on every iteration                       |\n   316\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   317\t| Transport-owned position anchors as jump targets                               | illegal for contract-generic code (Surfaces)                                | —                                                                                | rejected                                                                         |\n   318\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   319\t| Segment as batch value; pc indexes the loaded image                            | legal; the observer already delivers batches                                | one `next` per segment                                                           | **adopted**                                                                      |\n   320\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   321\t\n   322\tThe cursor-as-program-counter thesis of the streams note remains true where\n   323\tit applies: an execution *trace* emitted to a stream is a straight line, and\n   324\ta debugger's cursor over it is a pc in time (§3.6).\n   325\t\n   326\t### 3.3 Read outcomes at the two boundaries\n   327\t\n   328\t*Program boundary* (observer, generic): `ok` loads and runs; `blocked` ends\n   329\tthe round with the cursor retained; `end` ends the session; `gap` commits\n   330\tthe recovery cursor and counts the loss (an evicted segment is a program\n   331\tthat never ran; the REPL latch stays host policy); `cursor-mismatch`,\n   332\t`invalid-cursor`, `transport-error` throw naming the outcome. Unchanged from\n   333\tthe divergence register.\n   334\t\n   335\t*Data boundary* (instructions, via `engine/handle-effect`): identical to the\n   336\twalker because the handlers are shared.\n   337\t\n   338\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   339\t| Instruction      | `ok`                                 | `blocked`/`full`              | `end`       | `gap`                                                | terminal                                                  |\n   340\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   341\t| `:stream-next`   | `val ← value`, store cursor advanced | park with a wait entry (§3.5) | `val ← nil` | `val ← :dao.stream/gap`, cursor advanced to recovery | error naming outcome                                      |\n   342\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   343\t| `:stream-put`    | `val ← value`                        | park (`full`)                 | —           | —                                                    | `closed`, `invalid-value`, `transport-error` are errors   |\n   344\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   345\t| `:stream-cursor` | `val ← cursor-ref`                   | —                             | —           | —                                                    | `invalid-anchor`, `closed`, `transport-error` are errors  |\n   346\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   347\t| `:stream-make`   | `val ← stream-ref`                   | —                             | —           | —                                                    | `invalid-spec`, `not-found`, `transport-error` are errors |\n   348\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   349\t| `:stream-close`  | `val ← nil`                          | —                             | —           | —                                                    | `{ok}` only                                               |\n   350\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n   351\t\n   352\tUnder a ring-buffer composition `full` never occurs and loss surfaces as a\n   353\treader `gap`; the retention divergence stands as written.\n   354\t\n   355\t### 3.4 FFI through `dao.stream.apply`\n   356\t\n   357\t`:ffi-call` pops `argc` arguments and runs the semantic `park-and-call`:\n   358\tcheck the pair (`ffi/require-call-pair!`) **before** parking; park the frame\n   359\t`{:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call :segment :pc (pc+1) :env :stack :next\n   360\tk}`; `apply2/put-request!` on call-in; on `ok` add the call-out wait entry\n   361\tcorrelated by the parked id; on `full` retain the request in a\n   362\t`:request-sent` wait entry, as the walker does; the other outcomes fail the\n   363\tcall naming the outcome. The host side (`ffi/bridge-step`, `ffi/maybe-run`)\n   364\tis used unchanged. `call-result` and `call-response-wait-entry` are private\n   365\tin `ast_walker.cljc` today; Phase 0 lifts both into `yin.vm.ffi` so that\n   366\tcorrelation checking is written once (this is the one edit to an existing\n   367\tfile the roadmap requires).\n   368\t\n   369\t### 3.5 Scheduler, park, resume\n   370\t\n   371\tThe VM record carries the engine's scheduler fields (`:ready-queue`,\n   372\t`:wait-set`, `:parked`, `:id-counter`, `:store`, `:blocked?`, `:halted?`)\n   373\twith the same meanings. Wait entries built by the semantic `park-entry-fns`\n   374\tare `{:segment :pc :env :stack :k :reason :cursor-ref/:stream-id}`. The\n   375\trestore function is\n   376\t\n   377\t```\n   378\tsemantic-restore : base entry val → base with control {:segment :pc},\n   379\t                                       env, stack, k, val from entry\n   380\t```\n   381\t\n   382\tand is handed to `engine/check-wait-set`, `engine/resume-from-run-queue`,\n   383\t`engine/handle-effect`, and `engine/resume-continuation` exactly as\n   384\t`ast-walker-restore` is. `engine/run-loop` drives `run`. Nothing in\n   385\t`engine.cljc` changes.\n   386\t\n   387\t### 3.6 Observation and traces\n   388\t\n   389\tProgram observation is `stream-observer`, above the VM. For *execution*\n   390\tobservation the design reserves a trace vocabulary emitted at park points\n   391\tand, when enabled, per instruction:\n   392\t\n   393\t```\n   394\t[cfg :yin.trace/segment seg t] [cfg :yin.trace/pc pc t] [cfg :yin.trace/val v t]\n   395\t[cfg :yin.trace/env-ref e t]   [cfg :yin.trace/k-depth d t]\n   396\t```\n   397\t\n   398\twith `t` the step counter. This is the §6 endgame of the cesk-space plan:\n   399\tthe machine executes from the image and *emits* its configuration, so\n   400\t`as-of` and causal debugging are queries over a stream, not interpretation\n   401\tfrom one. `yin.vm.telemetry` is a stub that rejects a non-nil option; the\n   402\ttrace lands there when telemetry is designed, and nothing in Phase 1–3\n   403\tdepends on it. It is listed so the hot loop is written with the emission\n   404\tseam in one place (the `cesk-return` equivalent) rather than retrofitted.\n   405\t\n   406\t---\n   407\t\n   408\t## §4. CESK semantics on linear datoms\n   409\t\n   410\t### 4.1 Configuration\n   411\t\n   412\t$$\\langle C, E, S, K \\rangle \\quad\\text{where}\\quad\n   413\tC = \\langle seg, pc, val, St \\rangle$$\n   414\t\n   415\t- **C (control)**: the segment id, the program counter, the accumulator\n   416\t  `val`, and the operand stack `St` (a vector; `St` is control, not store,\n   417\t  because it is part of what the machine is *doing*, and it is saved in\n   418\t  frames).\n   419\t- **E (environment)**: a persistent map `sym → value`, extended on closure\n   420\t  entry with `merge closure-env (zipmap params args)`, as the walker does.\n   421\t  Lexical addressing is reserved (§6.4).\n   422\t- **S (store)**: the map `key → value` holding `yin/def` results, stream\n   423\t  handles, cursor entries, and the FFI pair — unchanged from `engine`.\n   424\t- **K (continuation)**: a vector of frames, innermost last. Frame kinds:\n   425\t  `{:type :return :segment :pc :env :stack-base}` for calls, and the\n   426\t  effect-continuation frames the engine already defines\n   427\t  (`:dao.stream.apply/eval-call`, `:request-sent`), extended with\n   428\t  `:segment :pc :stack`.\n   429\t\n   430\tLet `I = code[seg][pc]` be the decoded instruction and `I.x` its operands.\n   431\t\n   432\t### 4.2 Transitions for the core instructions\n   433\t\n   434\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n   435\t&\\text{const}:&& \\langle seg,pc,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,I.v,\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   436\t&\\text{var}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\rho(E,S,I.name),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   437\t&\\text{closure}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\mathrm{clo}(I.params,I.entry,seg,E),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   438\t&\\text{push}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,val,\\,St \\Vert [val],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   439\t&\\text{jump}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,I.target,\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   440\t&\\text{branch-false}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,(val\\ ?\\ pc{+}1 : I.target),\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   441\t&\\text{halt}:&& \\to \\text{halted},\\ \\text{result} = val\n   442\t\\end{aligned}$$\n   443\t\n   444\twhere $\\rho$ is `engine/resolve-var` (env → store → primitives → modules).\n   445\t\n   446\t**call** with $n = I.argc$, $St = St' \\Vert [f, a_1..a_n]$:\n   447\t\n   448\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n   449\t&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\ \\neg tail:&&\n   450\t\\to \\langle seg',\\,e,\\,val,\\,St',\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg,pc{+}1,E,|St'|)]\\rangle\\\\\n   451\t&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\ tail:&&\n   452\t\\to \\langle seg',\\,e,\\,val,\\,St',\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K\\rangle\\\\\n   453\t&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a)\\ \\text{a value } v:&&\n   454\t\\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,v,\\,St',E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   455\t&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a)\\ \\text{an effect } \\epsilon:&&\n   456\t\\to \\mathrm{effect}(\\epsilon, \\langle seg,pc{+}1,St',E,S,K\\rangle)\n   457\t\\end{aligned}$$\n   458\t\n   459\tA tail call grows neither $K$ nor $St$: the walker gets this for free\n   460\tbecause a closure body runs against the caller's $k$; the linear machine\n   461\tmust state it, and does. Effect handling `effect(ε, cfg)` is\n   462\t`engine/handle-effect`; its result is either a value (continue at `pc+1`)\n   463\tor a park (the configuration becomes a wait entry, control goes idle).\n   464\t\n   465\t**return** with $K = K' \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg',pc',E',b)]$:\n   466\t\n   467\t$$\\to \\langle seg',\\,pc',\\,val,\\,St[0..b),\\,E',\\,S,\\,K'\\rangle$$\n   468\t\n   469\tand with $K = []$: halted with result $val$.\n   470\t\n   471\t**stream-next** (cursor ref $c = val$), by outcome of `next` on the handle\n   472\tbehind $c$:\n   473\t\n   474\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n   475\t&ok\\ v,\\ c':&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,v,\\,St,E,\\,S[c \\mapsto c'],\\,K\\rangle\\\\\n   476\t&blocked:&& \\to \\text{parked}\\ \\{seg,pc{+}1,E,St,K,\\ \\text{cursor-ref}\\ c\\}\\ \\in\\ \\text{wait-set}\\\\\n   477\t&end:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\mathrm{nil},\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   478\t&gap\\ c':&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\text{:dao.stream/gap},\\,St,E,\\,S[c \\mapsto c'],\\,K\\rangle\n   479\t\\end{aligned}$$\n   480\t\n   481\t**ffi-call** ($St = St' \\Vert [a_1..a_n]$): park frame\n   482\t$\\{\\text{eval-call}, seg, pc{+}1, E, St', K\\}$ under a fresh id $p$; append\n   483\t$\\mathrm{request}(p, I.op, a)$ to call-in; on `ok` the wait set gains a\n   484\treader entry on the call-out cursor correlated by $p$; when the correlated\n   485\tresponse arrives, $val \\leftarrow$ its `ok` value (or its `error` raises)\n   486\tand control resumes at $pc{+}1$.\n   487\t\n   488\t**park**: $K$, $E$, $St$, $seg$, $pc{+}1$ are written to `:parked` under a\n   489\tfresh id; the VM halts with that record as its value.\n   490\t**resume** ($val$ = resume value, $I.id$ the parked id): the parked\n   491\tconfiguration is restored with $val$ as its accumulator.\n   492\t**current-continuation**: $val \\leftarrow \\{seg, pc{+}1, E, St, K\\}$\n   493\treified as data.\n   494\t\n   495\t### 4.3 What the equations make visible\n   496\t\n   497\tCompare the walker's evaluation of one operand: it allocates a frame map,\n   498\t`assoc`s the frame into $k$, `conj`s onto `:evaluated`, and dispatches on a\n   499\tkeyword `:type` twice (node, then continuation). The linear machine does\n   500\t`push` (one vector `conj`) and increments `pc`. The \"continuation\" of an\n   501\toperand sequence is the *next pc*, a fact the lowering already computed.\n   502\tThis is the entire performance argument, stated as semantics rather than as\n   503\tengineering (§6).\n   504\t\n   505\t---\n   506\t\n   507\t## §5. Lowering: Universal AST → linear datoms\n   508\t\n   509\t### 5.1 Placement and contract\n   510\t\n   511\t`yin.vm.linearize` (not `yang.linearize`): yang owns syntax → AST and must\n   512\tstay ignorant of any evaluator; lowering AST → executable code is VM lineage\n   513\tand must stay ignorant of any surface syntax. Clojure, Python and PHP front\n   514\tends all reach the linearizer through the one canonical form. The\n   515\trecommendation departs from the target-file example in the task brief for\n   516\tthis reason.\n   517\t\n   518\t```\n   519\tlower : [:yin/* datoms] → [:yin.code/* datoms]         ; pure, total over supported node types\n   520\tlower-ast : ast-map → [:yin.code/* datoms]             ; convenience = lower ∘ ast->datoms\n   521\t```\n   522\t\n   523\tInput is canonical AST datoms because that is what travels and what the\n   524\twalker consumes; the implementation may reconstruct maps with\n   525\t`vm/datoms->ast` internally in Phase 2 and lower from the datom index\n   526\tdirectly later. Output is one segment per program root.\n   527\t\n   528\t### 5.2 When: ahead-of-time at the load boundary\n   529\t\n   530\tCompilation is AOT per segment, once, where the program crosses into the\n   531\tVM: `eval` lowers its AST; a composition whose program stream carries AST\n   532\tdatoms composes `lower` into the loader it hands the observer; a composition\n   533\twhose stream carries code datoms hands the loader alone. There is no\n   534\ttiering, no recompile-at-boundary, no dirty flag: the engine's versioned\n   535\tcompile cache exists for programs that grow by append, and a segment is\n   536\timmutable once emitted. Streaming delivery of a growing segment (a pc past\n   537\tthe loaded length parks on code rather than erroring) is reserved for a\n   538\tlater phase; it is additive because a pc beyond `length` is already a\n   539\tdefined load-time condition.\n   540\t\n   541\t### 5.3 The flattening\n   542\t\n   543\tRecursive descent over the AST in evaluation order, emitting instructions\n   544\tinto a growing vector and lambda bodies out of line:\n   545\t\n   546\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   547\t| AST node                                                | Emitted sequence                                                   |\n   548\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   549\t| `:literal v`                                            | `const v`                                                          |\n   550\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   551\t| `:variable s`                                           | `var s`                                                            |\n   552\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   553\t| `:lambda ps body`                                       | `closure ps →L` ; body emitted later at label L ending in `return` |\n   554\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   555\t| `:application op args` (tail? τ)                        | `⟦op⟧ push ⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push call n τ`                         |\n   556\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   557\t| `:if t c a`                                             | `⟦t⟧ branch-false →A ⟦c⟧ jump →E A: ⟦a⟧ E:`                        |\n   558\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   559\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call op args`                        | `⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push ffi-call op n`                              |\n   560\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   561\t| `:stream/put target val`                                | `⟦target⟧ push ⟦val⟧ stream-put`                                   |\n   562\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   563\t| `:stream/cursor s`, `:stream/next s`, `:stream/close s` | `⟦s⟧ stream-cursor` etc.                                           |\n   564\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   565\t| `:stream/make b`                                        | `stream-make b`                                                    |\n   566\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   567\t| `:vm/store-get k`, `:vm/store-put k v`, `:vm/gensym p`  | one instruction each                                               |\n   568\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   569\t| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation`                  | one instruction each                                               |\n   570\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   571\t| `:vm/resume id v`                                       | `⟦v⟧ resume id`                                                    |\n   572\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   573\t| root                                                    | main sequence ends in `halt`; bodies follow                        |\n   574\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   575\t\n   576\tEvaluation order is the walker's (operator, then operands left to right),\n   577\tso effect order is identical, which the parity suite checks. Tail position\n   578\tis read from `:yin/tail?` as yang already marks it; `:if` branches inherit\n   579\tit; nothing new is inferred. Labels are resolved to instruction refs in a\n   580\tsecond pass; the linearizer emits refs, never pc integers.\n   581\t\n   582\tEvery instruction gets `:yin.code/source` = the AST node eid it came from,\n   583\tand the segment gets `:yin.code/derived-from` = the root eid. The reverse\n   584\tdirection (render an instruction range back to source) is a query, not a\n   585\tfunction in this namespace.\n   586\t\n   587\t---\n   588\t\n   589\t## §6. Performance architecture\n   590\t\n   591\t### 6.1 Traversal mechanics\n   592\t\n   593\tWalker, per AST node: read `:type` (keyword map lookup), destructure the\n   594\tnode (2–3 lookups), allocate a continuation frame, and later read the frame\n   595\tback (`:frame`, `:operands`, `:evaluated`, `:env`, `:next`: 5 lookups) and\n   596\tdispatch on `:type` of the frame. Nodes are heap-scattered persistent maps\n   597\treached by pointer from their parent.\n   598\t\n   599\tLinear machine, per instruction: `aget` on one array, one integer `case`,\n   600\tone operation on locals. Operands are fields of the decoded instruction\n   601\t(array slots or record fields), read once. The next instruction is the next\n   602\tarray slot.\n   603\t\n   604\t### 6.2 Memory and allocation\n   605\t\n   606\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n   607\t| Event                    | Walker allocations                         | Linear allocations                                     |\n   608\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n   609\t| evaluate an operand      | frame map, `assoc k`, `conj evaluated` (3) | `conj` onto `St` (1)                                   |\n   610\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n   611\t| begin an application     | frame map (1)                              | 0                                                      |\n   612\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n   613\t| test an `if`             | frame map (1)                              | 0                                                      |\n   614\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n   615\t| call a closure           | `zipmap`, `merge` (2)                      | `zipmap`, `merge`, return frame (3), 2 if tail         |\n   616\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n   617\t| every non-hot transition | one `ASTWalkerVM` record                   | 0 (registers stay in `loop` locals until a park point) |\n   618\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n   619\t\n   620\tFor `(+ x 1)` the walker performs roughly nine allocations and a dozen map\n   621\tlookups across six transitions; the linear machine performs three vector\n   622\t`conj`s and one argument vector across seven instructions. Call-heavy code\n   623\t(the countdown benchmark is nothing but calls and an `if`) is where the\n   624\tdifference lands. The remaining allocations are the persistent `St` and the\n   625\tenv `merge`; both have reserved optimizations (§6.4) that require no datom\n   626\tchange.\n   627\t\n   628\t### 6.3 Cache locality\n   629\t\n   630\tThe image is one contiguous array of small decoded records built in pc\n   631\torder, so sequential execution walks memory sequentially; a loop body is a\n   632\tfew cache lines revisited. Walker nodes are wherever the reader or\n   633\t`datoms->ast` allocated them, and each continuation frame is a fresh\n   634\tallocation on the nursery. On V8 and Dart, where keyword `case` compiles to\n   635\thash-and-compare chains and small maps are dictionary-mode objects, the\n   636\tinteger switch and array indexing matter more than on HotSpot; the historic\n   637\tbenchmarks put semantic bytecode at 2–7x slower on those hosts precisely\n   638\tbecause it did graph traversal and map lookups per step, both of which this\n   639\tdesign removes.\n   640\t\n   641\t### 6.4 Relation to bytecode, and what is reserved\n   642\t\n   643\tThe loaded image *is* bytecode. The difference from a traditional bytecode\n   644\tVM is only the stored form (five datoms per instruction rather than a few\n   645\tbytes) and the load step that decodes it. Execution cost is therefore\n   646\tbytecode-class by construction, and queryability is not paid for on the hot\n   647\tpath at all — it is paid once at load. The historic 1.0–1.1x register/stack\n   648\tline in `cesk-space-optimization.md` is the target; the walker sits at\n   649\t1.32x on that workload, the old graph-walking semantic VM at 1.69x.\n   650\t\n   651\tReserved, additive, none required for Phase 1–3:\n   652\t\n   653\t- **Lexical addressing**: `:var` gains `:yin.code/depth`/`:yin.code/index`\n   654\t  when the linearizer proves the name lexically bound; frames become vectors;\n   655\t  only unbound names walk store → primitives → modules.\n   656\t- **Superinstructions**: `:push-const`, `:push-var` fused by the loader (not\n   657\t  the linearizer, so datoms stay minimal).\n   658\t- **Host-array operand stack** inside the hot loop, materialized to a vector\n   659\t  only at park points (\"immutability observable only at park points\",\n   660\t  streams note §3).\n   661\t- **Content-addressed segments** (`:yin.code/hash`) for dedup across VMs and\n   662\t  for verifying a shipped continuation against its code.\n   663\t\n   664\tAcceptance in Phase 4 is measured, not asserted (§8).\n   665\t\n   666\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format\n   667\t\n   668\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state represents the canonical, architecture-agnostic continuation format for the `datom.world` ecosystem. \n   669\t\n   670\tBecause execution state is modeled as pure data rather than host memory (Axiom 4), the linear bytecode state serves as the universal *lingua franca* for network-transparent continuations. The lowered representation (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) resolves all execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST while remaining entirely decoupled from any specific hardware architecture.\n   671\t\n   672\t### 7.1 Cross-Architecture Normalization\n   673\tIf a highly specialized execution engine (e.g., a native WebAssembly compiler, a raw Register VM, or a hardware FPGA implementation) wishes to participate in the network, it must treat the Semantic VM state as the **normalized exchange format**:\n   674\t- **On Park (De-optimization):** When the specialized VM hits a blocking IO operation or a `:vm/park` instruction, it must map its specialized hardware registers or host stack back to the normalized linear format using the `:yin.code/source` mappings. It emits this standard Semantic VM continuation onto the stream.\n   675\t- **On Resume (Re-optimization):** When receiving a continuation off the network, the specialized VM reads the normalized `:pc` and `:stack`, lowers them into its own specialized hardware state, and resumes execution.\n   676\t\n   677\tBy enforcing the linear bytecode state as the universal standard, the network only ever trades simple integer PCs and array-based operand stacks. Any machine can pause execution on one continent, transmit the canonical state over a WebSocket, and resume execution on a radically different hardware architecture without losing semantic fidelity or performance.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
