{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '28,66p;320,370p;580,615p'\"","aggregated_output":"    28\t  is listening would be hidden global state.\n    29\t- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — \"do not introduce\n    30\t  callbacks; every callback is events on a stream\", seen from the stream's\n    31\t  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and\n    32\t  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).\n    33\t\n    34\tThe Invariants below are these denials in operational form.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n   320\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   321\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   322\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   323\t\n   324\tWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\n   325\tbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\n   326\tstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\n   327\tempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\n   328\tyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\n   329\tas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\n   330\tarrives as data.\n   331\t\n   332\tThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\n   333\tresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\n   334\tacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n   335\tdeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n   336\tnot implement `create!` — what it needs is a request whose result arrives\n   337\tlater, which is an ordinary interpreter over streams, not an operation here.\n   338\t\n   339\tCreation defers differently from attachment, and the difference is why this is\n   340\ta caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\n   341\tan answer only a far end can give, which is why it arrives as deposited data.\n   342\t`create!` has no far end. What it defers is **local acquisition**, whose\n   343\tfailure surfaces through the handle the caller already holds. A \"not yet\"\n   344\toutcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\n   345\tcannot operate, and nothing to retry against.\n   346\t\n   347\t### Host Dispatch\n   348\t\n   349\tWhen a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —\n   350\tfor example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on\n   351\t`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's\n   352\t`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:\n   353\t\n   354\t```clojure\n   355\t{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\n   356\t                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\n   357\t :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}\n   358\t```\n   359\t\n   360\tThe entries are per-transport and may be partial. A transport that creates\n   361\tno streams supplies no `:dao.stream/create` — the WebSocket transport owns\n   362\tno stream, it serves streams the host composed, so nothing there creates\n   363\tone. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and\n   364\treturns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and\n   365\tthe correct one: nothing here creates that.\n   366\t\n   367\tEntry values are **host-composed**: ordinarily closures produced by a\n   368\ttransport constructor that already received whatever composition state the\n   369\ttransport needs — a boundary's deposit destination and its admission\n   370\tdeclaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are\n   580\tother outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope\n   581\tis also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n   582\t\n   583\t`ok` is the local lifecycle transition, not a report on releasing host\n   584\tresources: what the handle is on is closed from that moment, irrevocably.\n   585\tA transport whose close must also flush or release something that can fail\n   586\tdeclares where that failure surfaces — an ordinary deposit on a channel it\n   587\tnames — because the outcome set has no room for it and the transition has\n   588\talready happened.\n   589\t\n   590\t`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle explicitly designated by\n   591\tthe transport as the logical stream's owner closes that logical stream; a\n   592\tserver-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain\n   593\townership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\n   594\thandle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\n   595\tstream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\n   596\tlater. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\n   597\tnature.\n   598\t\n   599\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   600\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   601\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   602\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   603\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   604\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   605\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   606\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   607\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   608\t\n   609\tClosing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\n   610\tappended to the logical stream afterwards are not observable through it, so\n   611\t`end` is reached at the position the stream had reached when the attachment\n   612\tclosed. And a logical stream that has itself been closed remains attachable —\n   613\t`attach!` answers `ok` and the new handle reads retained history to `end` —\n   614\tbecause close changes future availability, not identity or reachability.\n   615\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
