{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '346,387p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc;\"","aggregated_output":"(defn resume-from-run-queue\n  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge store-updates, and restore\n   VM-specific context.\"\n  [state restore-fn]\n  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n    (when (seq run-queue)\n      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n            base (assoc state\n                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n                        :store (merge (:store state) (:store-updates entry))\n                        :blocked? false\n                        :halted? false)]\n        (restore-fn base entry)))))\n\n\n(defn park-continuation\n  \"Add a parked continuation entry and halt the VM.\"\n  [state cont-fields]\n  (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n        park-id (keyword (str \"parked-\" id-counter))\n        parked (merge {:type :parked-continuation, :id park-id} cont-fields)]\n    (-> state\n        (update :parked assoc park-id parked)\n        (assoc :value parked\n               :halted? true\n               :id-counter (inc id-counter))\n        (telemetry/emit-snapshot :park {:parked-id park-id}))))\n\n\n(defn resume-continuation\n  \"Restore state from a parked continuation.\"\n  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\n  (if-let [parked (get-in state [:parked parked-id])]\n    (let [new-state (update state :parked dissoc parked-id)]\n      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\n          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :resume {:parked-id parked-id})))\n    (fail \"Cannot resume: parked continuation not found\"\n          {:parked-id parked-id})))\n\n\n(defn gensym\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '10,33p;76,92p;141,155p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '10,33p;76,92p;141,155p'\"","aggregated_output":"    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is events on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).\n    76\tdata; neither side holds a reference to the other.\n    77\t\n    78\tAn adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an\n    79\tinterpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the\n    80\teffect never appears as an emission.\n    81\t\n    82\tA callback is an event on a stream. That is the same rule in the other\n    83\tdirection: when the host initiates -- a socket receives, a connection drops, a\n    84\ttimer fires -- the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    85\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    86\t\n    87\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    88\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    89\t\n    90\t**An adapter is a map of those functions. That is all an adapter is.** It is a\n    91\tvalue, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows\n    92\tall deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The\n   141\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   142\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   143\t\n   144\t## Code Style\n   145\t\n   146\t- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n   147\t- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n   148\t- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n   149\t- Avoid cleverness.\n   150\t- Name things after what they do to streams.\n   151\t\n   152\t## Development Workflow\n   153\t\n   154\t- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n   155\t- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-13 17:24:28 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-14 00:24:28 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Verdict:** A canonical linear CESK exchange format does **not inherently violate Axiom 2**. Its data acquires execution semantics through a conforming interpreter; other observers remain free to query or render it. “Lift on park / lower on resume” is architecturally sound in principle, but Section 7 does not yet establish the contracts needed to guarantee heterogeneous migration.\n\nHigh means a defect blocking the stated migration guarantee; Medium means a contract or completion issue requiring clarification.\n\n| Severity | file:line | Invariant/evidence | Recommended correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High | [yin.vm.semantic.md:674](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:674) | **State reconstruction:** `:yin.code/source` identifies an AST node, not live register locations, inlined frames, operand values, or the exact suspension phase. Multiple instructions share a source node (§5.3), and hand-assembled instructions can omit it (§2.3). Source provenance cannot supply the specified inverse transformation. | Require compiler-generated safepoint reconstruction metadata tied to canonical segment/PC locations, including value materialization and frame reconstruction. Require resumed execution to preserve observable values and effect order. Keep source mappings as provenance. |\n| High | [yin.vm.semantic.md:670](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:670) | **Explicit state:** The proposed map is a local continuation fragment, not a self-contained CESK configuration. The store contains definitions, cursors and FFI bindings (§4.1); variable resolution also depends on supplied primitives/modules. Local restore receives an existing `base` (§3.5). Migrating the same map into a different base can change behavior. | Define the dependency closure: transferred immutable state, explicit external references, required primitive/module semantics, and suspension reason. State whether the accumulator is supplied by a resume event or captured. The entire scheduler need not migrate, but every required dependency must be accounted for. |\n| High | [yin.vm.semantic.md:670](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:670) | **Host isolation and portability:** “Pure data” contradicts §1.1’s explicit allowance for host functions and stream handles in environments/stacks (lines 44–48). DaoStream distinguishes host-local handles from portable descriptors. Integer PCs do not make arbitrary captured values portable across CLJ/CLJS/CLJD. | Define a recursive portable value encoding, including numeric semantics and closure/frame values. Encode primitive references symbolically and stream dependencies as descriptors plus observation state; attach through host interpreters. Return explicit unsupported outcomes for nonportable captures or unavailable resources. |\n| High | [yin.vm.semantic.md:674](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:674) | **Ownership, concurrency and linearization:** Publishing a parked value does not transfer execution ownership. No rule stops the source from waking while a receiver resumes, or multiple readers from resuming the same emission. FFI already distinguishes request admission from response waiting (§3.4); losing that phase can duplicate or skip effects. Local removal from `:parked` is not distributed arbitration. | Specify transfer versus intentional cloning, continuation identity, the ownership handoff point, duplicate handling and failure outcomes through explicit stream events. Preserve pending-effect phase, correlation identity and cursor state. Name the interpreter/composition owning this protocol; do not assign scheduling authority to the transport. |\n| High | [yin.vm.semantic.md:675](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:675) | **Code identity and dynamic extension:** A PC is meaningful only against exact code and interpreter semantics. Segment entities initially use remappable tempids (§2.1), while content addressing remains deferred (§6.4). The exchange contract has no version or required-feature declaration for evolving opcodes, frame layouts or primitive meanings. | Bind continuations—including nested closure/frame references—to immutable, unambiguous code identities and a versioned semantic profile. Declare required features and explicit incompatibility outcomes. Content hashing is one option; code-identity verification cannot remain unspecified once network migration is promised. |\n| Medium | [yin.vm.semantic.md:668](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:668) | **Interpretation boundary:** Canonical syntax is compatible with Axiom 2, but “resolves all execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST” attributes semantics to representation. §5.3 already obtains evaluation order from the walker’s interpretation. Hardware independence also does not establish equivalence with every possible VM semantics. | Describe the format as canonical for interpreters implementing a specified Yin execution profile. State that lowering records that interpretation’s chosen order and that specialized engines must preserve it. |\n| Medium | [yin.vm.semantic.md:677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:677) | **Completion criteria and migration risk:** “Without losing … performance” is unsupported: reconstruction, serialization, attachment and recompilation incur costs. The header says §7 remains elsewhere, and Phase 4 acceptance references an absent §8. Existing local-VM acceptance does not establish exchange compatibility. | Mark heterogeneous exchange as proposed until conformance criteria pass. Separate correctness guarantees from measured performance targets. Repair status/roadmap references and define compatibility with existing parked states. |\n\nThe following properties **passed architectural review**:\n\n- **Axiom 2:** Standardizing an exchange vocabulary does not make the stream its own interpreter or prevent independent interpretations.\n- **Layer ownership:** Syntax → AST remains with Yang; AST → executable datoms remains with the VM; loading and execution remain separate (§§1.2, 5.1).\n- **Explicit local control flow:** Segment/PC, operand stack and return frames expose sequencing and suspension state. Instruction fetch requires no additional stream boundary.\n- **Local state discipline:** Construction-supplied dependencies, immutable loaded images and persistent state do not introduce hidden globals or shared mutable state.\n- **Stream boundaries:** Emitting continuation data fits the no-callback architecture. WebSocket delivery can remain a host interpreter concern.\n- **Explicit reference construction:** The loader constructs branch/closure indexes and rejects dangling references.\n- **Specialized execution:** Host-specific registers and stacks are compatible with the axioms when they materialize a complete canonical state at defined suspension points.\n\nThe architectural defects are the insufficient reconstruction contract, incomplete migration dependencies, host-value contradiction, unspecified handoff semantics and unbound code/profile identity. Missing compiler adapters, codecs and conformance tests are **implementation gaps once those contracts exist**. Lexical addressing, superinstructions, host-array stacks and telemetry can remain **intentionally deferred**.\n\nCompletion should require lift/lower round trips at every supported suspension kind; cross-CLJ/CLJS/CLJD preservation of values and effect order; pending-FFI migration; duplicate delivery and source-wakeup races; and explicit rejection of incompatible code, features or resources. No files were edited; this was a read-only architecture review, not runtime certification."}}
